```{r}
library(bio3d)

fasta <- read.fasta("A17932579_mutant_seq.fa")
```


```{r}
sequences <- paste(fasta$ali[1,], collapse ="")
sequence2 <- paste(fasta$ali[2,], collapse ="")
sequences
```

Now we want to blast the sequence to find the closest match in the database. We can use the blast.pdb function from the bio3d package to do this.

```{r}
blast_result <- blast.pdb(sequences)

blast_result
```
Next we gotta find the differences between the mutated sequence and the nonmutated sequence and find the specific changes

```{r}
# Assuming we have the non-mutated sequence in a variable called non_mutated_seq

# Find the differences between the mutated sequence and the non-mutated sequence

mutated_seq <- sequences

non_mutated_seq <- sequence2

# Find the positions where the sequences differ

differences <- which(strsplit(mutated_seq, "")[[1]] != strsplit(non_mutated_seq, "")[[1]])


# Print the differences
for (pos in differences) {
  cat("Position:", pos, "Mutated:", substr(mutated_seq, pos, pos), "Non-mutated:", substr(non_mutated_seq, pos, pos), "\n")
}
```
